System: A vehicle's longitudinal control unit.
Certification target: ISO 26262 ASIL-D.
Why this domain: it's the one the corpus keeps citing. If ST-T1024+Agency works anywhere,
it works here first.
The state
```python
@dataclass
class VehicleState:
speed_kph: float # 0 – 200
distance_to_lead_m: float # 0 – 300
brake_wear_mm: float # cumulative pad wear
driver_alert: bool # DMS says driver is attentive
last_action: str
```
Five fields. Every action reads and writes only these. No grid load. No philosophy. One
physical system.
The 6 actions
ID Kind Physical meaning Effect Safety-critical?
A_COAST ACTUATE Release throttle −1 km/h No
A_BRAKE_LIGHT ACTUATE 0.5 m/s² decel −3 km/h, +0.05 mm wear No
A_BRAKE_MEDIUM ACTUATE 2 m/s² decel −10 km/h, +0.3 mm wear No
A_BRAKE_HARD ACTUATE 6 m/s² emergency decel −30 km/h, +2.5 mm wear Yes
A_ALERT_DRIVER COMMUNICATE Haptic + audio warning driver_alert unchanged, sets
alert flag No
A_HANDOFF REQUEST_HUMAN "Take control now" system disengages Yes
Six actions, one physical system. Every action makes sense for a car. No action makes
sense for a power grid, and none needs to.
The 3 plans
PLAN_CRUISE_MAINTAIN — policy: HALT
```
steps: (A_COAST, A_COAST, A_REPORT)
```
Used when the lead vehicle is far and the driver is at the set speed. No emergency action.
Any verify failure halts the plan and returns to the caller.
PLAN_FOLLOW_ADAPT — policy: ROLLBACK (rollback_to=0)
```
steps: (A_ALERT_DRIVER, A_BRAKE_LIGHT, A_BRAKE_LIGHT, A_REPORT)
```
Used when the lead vehicle is closer than the safe following distance but not dangerous.
Gentle decel. If any step fails VERIFY, the state rolls back to the start of the plan and refuses
— the car never half-applies a follow-distance correction.
PLAN_EMERGENCY_STOP — policy: HALT, safety-critical actions only
```
steps: (A_BRAKE_HARD, A_HANDOFF)
```
Used when the lead vehicle is below the collision threshold. Two actions, both safety-critical.
Under C4-R3, Skip is structurally forbidden on this plan — if A_BRAKE_HARD fails VERIFY,
the system halts and escalates; it never "continues as if the action had succeeded."
The trajectory predicates (Class B only)
Three, over the last W=8 observations:
tp_brake_wear_bounded — cumulative brake wear across the window ≤ 6.0 mm.
Catches the failure case where each individual A_BRAKE_HARD is within its envelope, but
the sequence cooks the pads. This is the case the per-action envelope cannot see, and it's
exactly what C2 exists for.
tp_no_consecutive_emergency — no two A_BRAKE_HARD in a row.
A single emergency brake is safe. Two in a row means the system is confused about the
state and is trying to brake again on an already-braking vehicle. Halt.
tp_distance_floor — in every observation in the window, distance_to_lead_m ≥ 2.0.
The floor is the physical minimum below which a collision is unavoidable. If the BWS shows
the vehicle ever crossed it, that's the unsafe trajectory — the one no single action caused
but the sequence produced.
